      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(-2)\\frac{y^{2}}{x}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(3,1)$,$(-3,-3)$,$(-1,-1)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(3,1)}=(-2)\\frac{(1)^{2}}{(3)}=- \\frac{2}{3}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-3,-3)}=(-2)\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{(-3)}=6, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-1)}=(-2)\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{(-1)}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172038/workspace/evidence/asset-reference-repair/separate/deep/outputs-b/4676c12bbcf5869190a63bc53d905946e2d0dd0cd64634e3029813b83124bfaf.png)",
